<onlyinclude>'''Semiconductor packaging''' is a coupled electrical, thermal and mechanical design problem spanning die interconnect, redistribution, substrate routing, environmental protection and board attachment. Integration density is not an independent objective: shorter links may reduce some parasitics while increasing heat flux, stress and test complexity.</onlyinclude>

'''Reading depth:''' [[Semiconductor packaging/Simple|Simple]] · [[Semiconductor packaging/Technical|Technical]]

== Physical and architectural model ==
A typical path comprises on-die pads, bumps or wire bonds, redistribution, package substrate and board-level contacts. The appropriate interconnect model depends on frequency and geometry: a lumped RLC approximation may suffice for electrically short paths, whereas high-speed links require transmission-line and electromagnetic analysis. A 2.5D package routes adjacent dies through an interposer or dense bridge; a 3D stack routes between vertically separated dies. Through-silicon vias are one possible 3D connection, not a defining requirement for every stack.

== Thermal co-design ==
A first-order steady-state approximation is
: ΔT = P × R<sub>θ</sub>
where ΔT is temperature rise in kelvin, P dissipated power in watts and R<sub>θ</sub> effective thermal resistance in K/W. For an illustrative 12 W device and a specified effective 3 K/W path, the predicted rise is 36 K. This is an arithmetic illustration, not a measured package or a guarantee of maximum junction temperature. Spatial hotspots, contact resistances, transient operation and coupling between adjacent dies invalidate a single uniform-resistance model.

== Thermomechanical and environmental reliability ==
Thermal expansion mismatch between silicon, copper, organic substrates and solder joints produces cyclic strain; interposer size, underfill properties and package warpage affect fatigue. Moisture absorbed by nonhermetic packages can induce delamination or cracking during solder reflow. IPC/JEDEC J-STD-020E provides a moisture/reflow sensitivity classification procedure, not a substitute for stress qualification or field-life prediction. Relevant tests include thermal cycling, moisture preconditioning, electrical continuity and application-specific functional tests. A passing sample is not proof of zero population failures.

== Measurement and validation workflow ==
For a particular package, specify the power map, ambient or case boundary condition, and thermal-interface properties before extracting a thermal resistance. Measure junction temperature with calibrated sensors or thermal test structures; compare transient measurements against finite-element predictions and report uncertainty. Characterize signal paths with frequency-domain S-parameters or time-domain eye diagrams at specified data rates, and correlate warpage and interconnect continuity across thermal cycling. These are methods to evaluate a design, not universal acceptance thresholds.

== Design tradeoffs and limitations ==
* Dense die-to-die interconnect can improve bandwidth per unit distance, but power delivery, crosstalk, signal integrity and repairability impose constraints.
* Thermal models must account for nonuniform power maps and temperature-dependent electrical parameters; finite-element and reduced-order models trade computational expense against fidelity.
* Interconnect and assembly yield, test coverage, known-good-die screening and package-level repair options affect cost.
* Labels such as “2.5D” and “3.5D” are not sufficient to infer a specific bond pitch, substrate material, cooling capacity or reliability level.

